WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.040
RECORDING: Laurel.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.611
Laurel.

00:00:03.611 --> 00:00:04.110
Laurel.

00:00:07.497 --> 00:00:08.455
DANA BOEBINGER: Laurel.

00:00:08.455 --> 00:00:10.420
KEVIN SITEK: Yanny.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.064
DANA BOEBINGER: It
just, it is Laurel.

00:00:12.064 --> 00:00:13.480
KEVIN SITEK: I've
listened to this

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.100
like so many different ways, and
all I can ever hear is Yanny.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.930
DANA BOEBINGER: So this is kind
of like the auditory version

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.030
of the dress that we
saw a few years ago

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:25.330
on the internet, in that it's
a physical stimulus that's

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:27.400
out there, and it's
kind of ambiguous.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.920
And depending on how
the brain is choosing

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.680
to interpret the signal,
you're going to perceive it

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.000
in a totally different way.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.540
So here we're looking
at a spectrogram

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:42.106
of the auditory illusion itself,
the Laurel or Yanny word.

00:00:42.106 --> 00:00:43.480
And what we're
actually seeing is

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.750
that on the x-axis in the
horizontal direction is time.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.970
And then the y-axis
is the frequency.

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:52.032
So low at bottom
and high at the top,

00:00:52.032 --> 00:00:54.490
and you can see that well it
sounds like a voice might only

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:57.100
be one pitch, There's
actually a whole bunch

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.060
of different frequencies
included in that speech sound.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.550
KEVIN SITEK: There's a
big gap on the lower side.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:07.470
It's not white, so there's
definitely some energy there,

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.090
some acoustic energy.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:13.960
And so what could be
happening is that people who

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:18.880
hear Laurel are actually
putting another line

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.734
through that space, whereas
people who hear Yanny,

00:01:23.734 --> 00:01:25.150
they're going to
say that whatever

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:28.720
is in between the two really
dark bands is actually just

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:29.960
like noise.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:30.490
DANA BOEBINGER:
Yeah, so it all has

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.614
to do with what frequencies
your brain is assigning

00:01:32.614 --> 00:01:35.350
are important for figuring
out which speech sound it is.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:36.807
KEVIN SITEK: So
like this is what

00:01:36.807 --> 00:01:38.890
it sounds like if you cut
out the high frequencies

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.550
and only have the
low frequencies.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:41.767
RECORDING: Laurel.

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:43.100
KEVIN SITEK: I even hear Laurel.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:43.980
INTERVIEWER: I heard Laurel.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:44.830
DANA BOEBINGER:
Yeah, that's Laurel.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:45.400
INTERVIEWER: That's Laurel.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:46.399
KEVIN SITEK: Definitely.

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:49.720
And if you cut out the low
frequencies and you only

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.732
have the high frequencies--

00:01:51.732 --> 00:01:53.795
RECORDING: Yanny.

00:01:53.795 --> 00:01:54.920
KEVIN SITEK: Is that Yanny?

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:55.880
DANA BOEBINGER: That's Yanny.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.630
KEVIN SITEK: The same
acoustic information

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.390
is hitting everyone's ears.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:03.480
But the brain is then going
to interpret that differently.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:06.500
So that's based on
the brain's experience

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.340
with bad phone speakers, based
on the brain's experience

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.980
with this language,
and also, for instance,

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.394
some people with high
frequency hearing loss,

00:02:16.394 --> 00:02:17.810
like as people get
older, you tend

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:20.290
to lose some of the
highest frequencies.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:23.540
Yeah I think it's probably
good to have some friends

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:24.930
on the Laurel side, just to--

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.638
DANA BOEBINGER: That's
right, we all need

00:02:26.638 --> 00:02:28.250
friends in Laurel places.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.600
[LAUGHS]

